  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nUpper heights will increase Wednesday on the order of ~5 DM at H500. \nThis will help to keep the best convective chances west of the FA, \nbut lower thicknesses will allow highs to be cooler than Tuesday and \naverage near 90 across our northwest to around 100 across our \nsoutheast. Better rain chances will work back into the forecast by \nlate week and the weekend. The upper high over the southeast CONUS \nwill weaken from H600 to H594, brought on by both a weak upper level \nlow/inverted trough that will move into the Central Gulf Coast by \nSaturday, and by an upper trough formed from the remnants of a \nprogged upper low over the Pacific Northwest. The aforementioned \nupper trough should help to bring monsoonal moisture over the \nSouthern Plains and hopefully bring much needed rainfall.  A \npossible cold front by late weekend would help to enhance lift and, \nas a result, convection. Jackets and coats may be needed by early \nnext week if the current forecast continues to hold as highs will \nonly reach into the low 80s.",
